    <main class="deck q-deck" aria-label="Q methodology slide deck">
<section class="slide is-active" data-title="Q methodology" aria-hidden="false"><div class="slide-inner title-slide">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Postgraduate workshop · November 2026</span></header>
<div class="title-rule"></div>
<div data-language="en"><h1>Q methodology</h1><p class="lead">Understanding shared viewpoints</p><p class="q-cover-meta">April Jiawei Zhang</p><p>What does Q study? How do we design, sort and interpret a Q study?</p><div class="q-cover-sort" aria-label="Example sorting grid with one, two, three, two and one spaces"><div><b>−2</b><span></span></div><div><b>−1</b><span></span><span></span></div><div><b>0</b><span></span><span></span><span></span></div><div><b>+1</b><span></span><span></span></div><div><b>+2</b><span></span></div></div></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h1>Q 方法</h1><p class="lead">理解共同觀點</p><p class="q-cover-meta">April Jiawei Zhang 張家維</p><p>Q研究甚麼？如何設計、排序及詮釋一項Q研究？</p></div>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="What Q does: sort, compare, explain" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner"><header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Start here</span></header>
<div data-language="en"><h2>People sort cards. We look for shared viewpoints.</h2><p class="lead">Q methodology helps us discover different ways people think about the same topic.</p><div class="q-two"><article><h3>What the participant does</h3><p>Read a set of opinion statements. Arrange them from “most disagree” to “most agree”. Explain the choices.</p><p>The finished arrangement is a <strong>Q-sort</strong>: one person’s viewpoint expressed through the cards.</p></article><article><h3>What the researcher does</h3><p>Compare everyone’s complete arrangements. Use statistical analysis to find similar patterns, then explain what each pattern means.</p><p>A shared pattern is called a <strong>factor</strong>: a shared viewpoint.</p></article></div><p class="q-takeaway">The result is a description of different viewpoints. A typical small, purposefully selected Q study cannot tell us how common each viewpoint is in the whole population.</p><p class="source-note">Developed by William Stephenson in 1935. “Subjectivity” means people’s own viewpoints. Q combines numerical comparison with interpretation of people’s explanations.</p><p class="source-note"><a href="https://qmethod.org/resources/how2q/">Q Methodology Society · Getting started with Q</a></p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>參與者排卡片，研究者找共同觀點。</h2><p class="lead">Q方法幫助我們發現：面對同一議題，人們有哪些不同的理解方式？</p><div class="q-two"><article><h3>參與者做甚麼？</h3><p>閱讀一組意見陳述，從「最不同意」排至「最同意」，再解釋選擇。</p><p>完成的排列稱為<strong>Q排序（Q-sort）</strong>，用卡片呈現一個人的觀點。</p></article><article><h3>研究者做甚麼？</h3><p>比較所有人的完整排列，用統計分析找出相似模式，再解釋每個模式的意義。</p><p>共同模式稱為<strong>因素（factor）</strong>，代表一種共同觀點。</p></article></div><p class="q-takeaway">研究結果描述不同觀點。典型的小型立意取樣Q研究，不能告訴我們每種觀點在全體人口中有多普遍。</p><p class="source-note">William Stephenson於1935年提出Q方法。「主觀性」指人們自己的觀點。Q結合數字比較與參與者解釋的詮釋。</p><p class="source-note"><a href="https://qmethod.org/resources/how2q/">Q Methodology Society · Getting started with Q</a></p></div>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="The seven steps: an IDLE example" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner"><header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">The whole journey</span></header>
<div data-language="en"><h2>One question. Seven steps.</h2><p><strong>Our example: informal digital learning of English (IDLE).</strong> This means choosing to use English through videos, games, social media or online communities outside formal lessons.</p><blockquote>What different viewpoints do university students hold about learning and using English online outside class?</blockquote><ol class="q-roadmap"><li><strong>Gather different opinions</strong><span>Concourse</span></li><li><strong>Choose the statement cards</strong><span>Q set</span></li><li><strong>Invite people with varied experiences</strong><span>P set</span></li><li><strong>Ask each person to arrange the cards</strong><span>Q sorting</span></li><li><strong>Ask why they placed the cards there</strong><span>Post-sort interview</span></li><li><strong>Find similar whole arrangements</strong><span>By-person factor analysis</span></li><li><strong>Explain and name each shared viewpoint</strong><span>Interpretation</span></li></ol><p class="q-takeaway">Start with a question about viewpoints. We will follow the same IDLE example through all seven steps.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>一個問題，七個步驟。</h2><p><strong>貫穿例子：非正式數碼英語學習（IDLE）。</strong>指在正式課堂以外，自主透過影片、遊戲、社交媒體或網上社群接觸與使用英語。</p><blockquote>大學生對課堂以外的網上英語學習與使用，持有哪些不同觀點？</blockquote><ol class="q-roadmap"><li><strong>蒐集不同意見</strong><span>論述總體 concourse</span></li><li><strong>選出排序用的陳述卡</strong><span>Q陳述集 Q set</span></li><li><strong>邀請具不同經驗的人</strong><span>P參與者集 P set</span></li><li><strong>請每人排列卡片</strong><span>Q排序 Q sorting</span></li><li><strong>詢問這樣排列的原因</strong><span>排序後訪談</span></li><li><strong>找出相似的完整排列</strong><span>以人為中心的因素分析</span></li><li><strong>解釋並命名共同觀點</strong><span>詮釋</span></li></ol><p class="q-takeaway">先提出關於觀點的研究問題，再用同一個IDLE例子走過七個步驟。</p></div>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="1. An interview question and three sample answers" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner"><header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Step 1 · Collect opinions</span></header><div data-language="en"><h2>We begin by asking learners about their experience.</h2><blockquote>“Tell me about a recent time you used English online outside class. What mattered to you?”</blockquote><div class="q-table-scroll"><table class="q-worked-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Example learner comment</th><th scope="col">What we learn</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">“I watch English clips because they are fun.”</th><td>Enjoyment matters.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">“I pause videos and write down useful expressions.”</th><td>Some learners deliberately study.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">“I want to chat, but I worry about making mistakes.”</th><td>Participation can feel difficult.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Ask follow-up questions: “Can you give an example?” and “What makes that easy or difficult?” Add contrasting views from discussions and research.</p><p class="q-takeaway">Save the comments and their sources. This growing pool of opinions is the <strong>concourse</strong>.</p><p class="source-note">Invented IDLE example for learning the procedure. These are not interview data or study findings.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>先請學習者談一次具體經驗。</h2><blockquote>「請談談最近一次你在課堂以外上網用英語的經驗。甚麼對你最重要？」</blockquote><div class="q-table-scroll"><table class="q-worked-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">學習者的假設回答</th><th scope="col">我們看到的想法</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">「英語短片好看，所以我會看。」</th><td>享受內容很重要。</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">「我會暫停影片，記下有用的表達。」</th><td>有些人會刻意學習。</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">「我想聊天，但擔心犯錯。」</th><td>參與交流可能令人不安。</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>再問：「可以舉個例子嗎？」及「甚麼令這件事容易或困難？」也從討論及研究中蒐集不同看法。</p><p class="q-takeaway">保存意見及來源。這個逐漸擴大的意見庫，就是<strong>論述總體（concourse）</strong>。</p><p class="source-note">為學習程序而設的IDLE假設例子，並非訪談資料或研究結果。</p></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="2. Turn a comment into one clear statement" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner"><header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Step 2 · Write the cards</span></header><div data-language="en"><h2>A comment becomes a statement someone can rank.</h2><div class="q-table-scroll"><table class="q-worked-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Collected idea</th><th scope="col">Possible card wording</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Videos are enjoyable.</th><td>“Enjoyment is my main reason for engaging with English-language content online.”</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">I stop to study useful language.</th><td>“I deliberately study new expressions I encounter online.”</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">I worry when joining a conversation.</th><td>“I feel confident joining online conversations in English.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The third card lets people express confidence or difficulty through their placement. Check the wording with learners to make sure it captures the intended idea.</p><p class="q-takeaway">Each card expresses one opinion. A card is something to agree or disagree with, not a question with a correct answer.</p><p class="source-note">Invented IDLE example for learning the procedure. These are not interview data or study findings.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>把訪談想法寫成可以排序的陳述。</h2><div class="q-table-scroll"><table class="q-worked-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">蒐集到的想法</th><th scope="col">可用的卡片措辭</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">影片內容很有趣。</th><td>「享受內容是我在網上接觸英語的主要原因。」</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">我會停下來學習有用的語言。</th><td>「我會刻意學習在網上遇到的新表達。」</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">參與對話時會擔心。</th><td>「我有信心參與網上的英語對話。」</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>第三張卡讓人透過排序表達自信或困難。請學習者試讀，確認措辭保留了原來想法。</p><p class="q-takeaway">每張卡只表達一個意見，讓人表示同意或不同意。卡片不是有標準答案的考題。</p><p class="source-note">為學習程序而設的IDLE假設例子，並非訪談資料或研究結果。</p></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="2. Check coverage and try the cards with a learner" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner"><header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Step 2 · Check the card set</span></header><div data-language="en"><h2>The cards need to offer genuinely different positions.</h2><div class="q-two"><article><h3>Check what is covered</h3><p>Lay the candidate cards under themes: enjoyment, deliberate learning, social connection, confidence, translation and access.</p><p>Look for repeated ideas and missing views. Keep a record of why each card stays or goes.</p></article><article><h3>Try the task with a learner</h3><p>Say: “Please read this card aloud in your own words. What does it mean to you?”</p><p>Then try a complete sort. Ask which cards were unclear or hard to place and what important view was missing. Revise and try again.</p></article></div><p class="q-takeaway">You now have a numbered set of cards for everyone to sort: the <strong>Q set</strong>. Our nine cards demonstrate the task. A research Q set needs its own justification and pilot.</p><p class="source-note"><a href="https://qmethod.org/resources/how2q/">Q Methodology Society: videos, readings and workshops</a></p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>卡片要讓不同立場都有表達空間。</h2><div class="q-two"><article><h3>檢查涵蓋哪些想法</h3><p>把候選卡按主題排開：享受、刻意學習、社群連繫、自信、翻譯及使用條件。</p><p>找出重複想法及遺漏觀點，記錄保留或刪除每張卡的理由。</p></article><article><h3>請學習者試做</h3><p>問：「請用自己的話說說這張卡的意思。你怎樣理解它？」</p><p>再試做一次完整排序，詢問哪些卡不清楚、難放，或缺少甚麼重要看法。修改後再試。</p></article></div><p class="q-takeaway">最後得到所有人共同排序、附有編號的卡片組：<strong>Q陳述集（Q set）</strong>。本課九張卡用來示範程序，研究用陳述集需另外論證及試行。</p><p class="source-note"><a href="https://qmethod.org/resources/how2q/">Q Methodology Society: videos, readings and workshops</a></p></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="3. A concrete recruitment plan" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner"><header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Step 3 · Invite participants</span></header><div data-language="en"><h2>We invite learners who may see the topic differently.</h2><div class="q-table-scroll"><table class="q-worked-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Whom we seek in this example</th><th scope="col">Why include them?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Students who mainly watch English videos</th><td>They may value enjoyment or exposure.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Students who game or chat in English</th><td>They may value interaction.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Students who deliberately study online language</th><td>They may value intentional learning.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Students who participate less or face access difficulties</th><td>They may bring views active users miss.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These are recruitment starting points. Ask about actual experience and allow unexpected viewpoints to emerge.</p><p class="q-takeaway">The people who take part are the <strong>P set</strong>. Explain the study, obtain consent and ask each person to sort independently. Different recruitment categories do not automatically become factors.</p><p class="source-note">Invented IDLE example for learning the procedure. These are not interview data or study findings.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>邀請可能有不同看法的學習者。</h2><div class="q-table-scroll"><table class="q-worked-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">本例希望邀請誰？</th><th scope="col">為甚麼納入？</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">主要觀看英語影片的學生</th><td>可能重視享受或接觸英語。</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">用英語遊戲或聊天的學生</th><td>可能重視交流。</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">刻意學習網上語言的學生</th><td>可能重視有意識的學習。</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">較少參與或使用條件受限的學生</th><td>可能帶來活躍使用者未提到的看法。</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>這些只是招募起點，需詢問實際經驗，並容許未預料的觀點出現。</p><p class="q-takeaway">參與的人就是<strong>P參與者集（P set）</strong>。說明研究、取得知情同意，讓每人獨立排序。招募類別不會自動變成因素。</p><p class="source-note">為學習程序而設的IDLE假設例子，並非訪談資料或研究結果。</p></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="4. What the researcher says before sorting" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner"><header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Step 4 · Explain the task</span></header><div data-language="en"><h2>Here is what you can say to a participant.</h2><blockquote>“Think about your own experience of using English online outside class. Read all nine cards. Arrange them from most disagree to most agree. Compare the cards with one another. You can move them until the whole arrangement fits your view.”</blockquote><div class="q-two"><article><h3>Explain the spaces</h3><p>−2: one card<br />−1: two cards<br />0: three cards<br />+1: two cards<br />+2: one card</p></article><article><h3>Check understanding</h3><p>Ask: “If you agree with several cards, what would you do?”</p><p>Expected understanding: compare them, put the strongest agreement furthest right, then arrange the others relative to it.</p></article></div><p class="q-takeaway">Everyone receives the same sorting question. The researcher explains the task and lets the participant decide where the cards belong.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>研究者可以這樣說明任務。</h2><blockquote>「根據你在課堂以外上網用英語的自身經驗，讀完九張卡，再從最不同意排至最同意。卡片要互相比較。你可以一直移動，直到整份排列符合你的觀點。」</blockquote><div class="q-two"><article><h3>解釋每欄格數</h3><p>−2：一張<br />−1：兩張<br />0：三張<br />+1：兩張<br />+2：一張</p></article><article><h3>確認對方理解</h3><p>問：「如果你同意好幾張卡，你會怎樣做？」</p><p>應理解為：把它們互相比較，最同意的放最右，其餘按相對程度排列。</p></article></div><p class="q-takeaway">所有人使用同一排序問題。研究者解釋任務，卡片位置由參與者決定。</p></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="4. A complete worked example before you try" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner"><header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Step 4 · Watch an example</span></header><div data-language="en"><h2>Imagine one learner who values connection and study.</h2><div class="q-two"><article><h3>First: the strongest agreement</h3><p>They put card 1 at +2: “Using English online makes me feel part of a wider community.”</p></article><article><h3>Then: the strongest disagreement</h3><p>They put card 4 at −2: “Enjoyment is my main reason…” fits them least well. For this learner, connection and study matter more.</p></article></div><details class="q-method-detail"><summary>Show how this learner placed all nine cards</summary><div class="q-demo-grid"><div><h3>-2</h3><div class="q-demo-card"><b>4</b><span>Enjoyment is my main reason for engaging with English-language content online.</span></div></div><div><h3>-1</h3><div class="q-demo-card"><b>5</b><span>Limited access to suitable digital resources restricts my English activities.</span></div><div class="q-demo-card"><b>9</b><span>I rely on translation tools to understand English-language content online.</span></div></div><div><h3>0</h3><div class="q-demo-card"><b>3</b><span>Choosing my own digital content helps me stay engaged with English.</span></div><div class="q-demo-card"><b>7</b><span>I feel confident joining online conversations in English.</span></div><div class="q-demo-card"><b>8</b><span>I see myself as an English learner during my digital leisure activities.</span></div></div><div><h3>+1</h3><div class="q-demo-card"><b>2</b><span>I use English online to build relationships with people who share my interests.</span></div><div class="q-demo-card"><b>6</b><span>I deliberately study new expressions I encounter online.</span></div></div><div><h3>+2</h3><div class="q-demo-card"><b>1</b><span>Using English online makes me feel part of a wider community.</span></div></div></div></details><p>They put cards 2 and 6 at +1 because relationships and deliberate study matter. They review the remaining cards and fill the other spaces.</p><p class="q-takeaway">This is one possible sort. Your arrangement can be different. The important point is to express your own view across all nine cards.</p><p class="source-note">Invented IDLE example for learning the procedure. These are not interview data or study findings.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>想像一位重視連繫及學習的學生。</h2><div class="q-two"><article><h3>先放最同意的一張</h3><p>把卡片1放+2：「在網上使用英語讓我覺得自己是更廣泛社群的一分子。」</p></article><article><h3>再放最不同意的一張</h3><p>把卡片4放−2，因為「享受內容是主要原因」最不符合自己的看法。對這位學生而言，連繫與學習更重要。</p></article></div><details class="q-method-detail"><summary>查看這位學生的完整九張卡排列</summary><div class="q-demo-grid"><div><h3>-2</h3><div class="q-demo-card"><b>4</b><span>享受內容是我在網上接觸英語的主要原因。</span></div></div><div><h3>-1</h3><div class="q-demo-card"><b>5</b><span>缺乏合適的數碼資源限制了我的英語活動。</span></div><div class="q-demo-card"><b>9</b><span>我依靠翻譯工具理解網上的英語內容。</span></div></div><div><h3>0</h3><div class="q-demo-card"><b>3</b><span>自行選擇數碼內容有助我持續接觸英語。</span></div><div class="q-demo-card"><b>7</b><span>我有信心參與網上的英語對話。</span></div><div class="q-demo-card"><b>8</b><span>進行數碼休閒活動時，我視自己為英語學習者。</span></div></div><div><h3>+1</h3><div class="q-demo-card"><b>2</b><span>我在網上使用英語，與興趣相近的人建立關係。</span></div><div class="q-demo-card"><b>6</b><span>我會刻意學習在網上遇到的新表達。</span></div></div><div><h3>+2</h3><div class="q-demo-card"><b>1</b><span>在網上使用英語讓我覺得自己是更廣泛社群的一分子。</span></div></div></div></details><p>把卡片2及6放+1，因為建立關係及刻意學習很重要。再比較其餘卡片，填滿其他位置。</p><p class="q-takeaway">這只是一種可能的排序，你的排列可以不同。重點是用九張卡一起表達自己的觀點。</p><p class="source-note">為學習程序而設的IDLE假設例子，並非訪談資料或研究結果。</p></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="Try a Q sort" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Step 4 · Arrange the cards</span></header>

<div data-language="en"><h2>Try a nine-statement Q sort</h2><p class="q-sort-instruction">Think about your own experience of using English online outside class. There are no right or wrong viewpoints.</p><ol class="q-sort-steps"><li><strong>Read all nine cards.</strong> Think which you agree with, disagree with or feel unsure about.</li><li><strong>Place the two extremes.</strong> Put your most agreed-with card at +2 and your most disagreed-with card at −2. Each end holds one card.</li><li><strong>Place the remaining seven.</strong> Put two at +1, two at −1 and three at 0. Compare cards with one another; 0 can mean mixed feelings or lower importance.</li><li><strong>Review and explain.</strong> Move cards until the whole arrangement fits your view, then click “Check my sort”.</li></ol><div class="q-sort-workspace"><div class="q-statements" data-sort-bank><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="1" aria-pressed="false"><b>1</b><span>Using English online makes me feel part of a wider community.</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="2" aria-pressed="false"><b>2</b><span>I use English online to build relationships with people who share my interests.</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="3" aria-pressed="false"><b>3</b><span>Choosing my own digital content helps me stay engaged with English.</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="4" aria-pressed="false"><b>4</b><span>Enjoyment is my main reason for engaging with English-language content online.</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="5" aria-pressed="false"><b>5</b><span>Limited access to suitable digital resources restricts my English activities.</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="6" aria-pressed="false"><b>6</b><span>I deliberately study new expressions I encounter online.</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="7" aria-pressed="false"><b>7</b><span>I feel confident joining online conversations in English.</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="8" aria-pressed="false"><b>8</b><span>I see myself as an English learner during my digital leisure activities.</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="9" aria-pressed="false"><b>9</b><span>I rely on translation tools to understand English-language content online.</span></button></div><div class="q-sort-board"><div class="q-rank-ends"><span>Most disagree</span><span>Most agree</span></div><div class="q-rank-grid"><div class="q-rank-column"><b>-2</b><small data-rank-count="-2">0 / 1</small><div class="q-slots" data-rank-slots="-2" aria-label="Statements at -2"><span class="q-slot">—</span></div></div><div class="q-rank-column"><b>-1</b><small data-rank-count="-1">0 / 2</small><div class="q-slots" data-rank-slots="-1" aria-label="Statements at -1"><span class="q-slot">—</span><span class="q-slot">—</span></div></div><div class="q-rank-column"><b>0</b><small data-rank-count="0">0 / 3</small><div class="q-slots" data-rank-slots="0" aria-label="Statements at 0"><span class="q-slot">—</span><span class="q-slot">—</span><span class="q-slot">—</span></div></div><div class="q-rank-column"><b>+1</b><small data-rank-count="1">0 / 2</small><div class="q-slots" data-rank-slots="1" aria-label="Statements at +1"><span class="q-slot">—</span><span class="q-slot">—</span></div></div><div class="q-rank-column"><b>+2</b><small data-rank-count="2">0 / 1</small><div class="q-slots" data-rank-slots="2" aria-label="Statements at +2"><span class="q-slot">—</span></div></div></div><p class="q-sort-hint">Drag a card into an empty space, or click a card and then a space. Drop onto another card to swap. To take a card out, select it and click “Return selected card”.</p><div class="q-actions"><button type="button" class="secondary" data-return-sort>Return selected card</button><button type="button" data-check-sort>Check my sort</button><button type="button" class="secondary" data-reset-sort>Reset sort</button></div><p class="q-feedback" data-sort-feedback role="status"></p><p class="source-note">Teaching example only. Choices stay in this browser and are not submitted. If you have no relevant experience, observe a partner. The grid fixes the number of spaces; it does not mean opinions follow a normal distribution.</p></div></div></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>試做九項陳述的Q排序</h2><p class="q-sort-instruction">根據你在課堂以外使用網上英語的自身經驗作答，觀點沒有對錯。</p><ol class="q-sort-steps"><li><strong>讀完九張卡。</strong>先想想哪些同意、不同意或未能確定。</li><li><strong>先放兩端。</strong>最同意的一張放+2，最不同意的一張放−2；兩端各放一張。</li><li><strong>再放餘下七張。</strong>+1及−1各放兩張，0放三張。卡片要互相比較；0可代表感受不一或重要性較低。</li><li><strong>檢查並解釋。</strong>移動卡片至整體排列符合你的觀點，再按「檢查排序」。</li></ol><div class="q-sort-workspace"><div class="q-statements" data-sort-bank><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="1" aria-pressed="false"><b>1</b><span>在網上使用英語讓我覺得自己是更廣泛社群的一分子。</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="2" aria-pressed="false"><b>2</b><span>我在網上使用英語，與興趣相近的人建立關係。</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="3" aria-pressed="false"><b>3</b><span>自行選擇數碼內容有助我持續接觸英語。</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="4" aria-pressed="false"><b>4</b><span>享受內容是我在網上接觸英語的主要原因。</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="5" aria-pressed="false"><b>5</b><span>缺乏合適的數碼資源限制了我的英語活動。</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="6" aria-pressed="false"><b>6</b><span>我會刻意學習在網上遇到的新表達。</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="7" aria-pressed="false"><b>7</b><span>我有信心參與網上的英語對話。</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="8" aria-pressed="false"><b>8</b><span>進行數碼休閒活動時，我視自己為英語學習者。</span></button><button type="button" class="q-statement" draggable="true" data-sort-id="9" aria-pressed="false"><b>9</b><span>我依靠翻譯工具理解網上的英語內容。</span></button></div><div class="q-sort-board"><div class="q-rank-ends"><span>最不同意</span><span>最同意</span></div><div class="q-rank-grid"><div class="q-rank-column"><b>-2</b><small data-rank-count="-2">0 / 1</small><div class="q-slots" data-rank-slots="-2" aria-label="陳述位置 -2"><span class="q-slot">—</span></div></div><div class="q-rank-column"><b>-1</b><small data-rank-count="-1">0 / 2</small><div class="q-slots" data-rank-slots="-1" aria-label="陳述位置 -1"><span class="q-slot">—</span><span class="q-slot">—</span></div></div><div class="q-rank-column"><b>0</b><small data-rank-count="0">0 / 3</small><div class="q-slots" data-rank-slots="0" aria-label="陳述位置 0"><span class="q-slot">—</span><span class="q-slot">—</span><span class="q-slot">—</span></div></div><div class="q-rank-column"><b>+1</b><small data-rank-count="1">0 / 2</small><div class="q-slots" data-rank-slots="1" aria-label="陳述位置 +1"><span class="q-slot">—</span><span class="q-slot">—</span></div></div><div class="q-rank-column"><b>+2</b><small data-rank-count="2">0 / 1</small><div class="q-slots" data-rank-slots="2" aria-label="陳述位置 +2"><span class="q-slot">—</span></div></div></div><p class="q-sort-hint">把卡拖至空格，或先點卡片再點空格。放到另一張卡上可交換；要取出卡片，先選卡，再按「移回所選卡片」。</p><div class="q-actions"><button type="button" class="secondary" data-return-sort>移回所選卡片</button><button type="button" data-check-sort>檢查排序</button><button type="button" class="secondary" data-reset-sort>重設排序</button></div><p class="q-feedback" data-sort-feedback role="status"></p><p class="source-note">僅供教學。選擇只保留於此瀏覽器，並不提交；沒有相關經驗者可觀察同伴。格網規定每欄的格數，不表示意見服從常態分布。</p></div></div></div>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="Step 5: ask why the cards went there" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Step 5 · Ask why</span></header>

<div data-language="en"><h2>Ask why they put the cards there.</h2><p>This is the <strong>post-sort interview</strong>: a conversation after the card arrangement. Ask open questions and record the participant’s own explanation.</p><div class="q-two"><article><h3>Your +2 statement</h3><p data-endpoint="2">Complete the sort to see your statement here.</p><p>Why does this statement best express your view?</p></article><article><h3>Your −2 statement</h3><p data-endpoint="-2">Complete the sort to see your statement here.</p><p>Why does this statement fit your view least well?</p></article></div><p class="lead small">Which choice was difficult? What did the middle mean? Was an important view missing?</p><p class="q-takeaway">You finish with a saved arrangement and the participant’s reasons. Positions are relative within a person’s sort. A negative score can mean less agreement than with the other statements; zero can reflect ambivalence or lower salience.</p><p><strong>Worked conversation:</strong> Researcher: “Why is card 1 at +2?” Learner: “I feel part of a gaming group when we use English.” Researcher: “Can you describe a time that happened?” Record the learner’s reason with their participant code. This dialogue is invented.</p><p class="source-note">van Exel &amp; de Graaf (2005)</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>詢問：為甚麼把卡放在這裡？</h2><p>這就是<strong>排序後訪談</strong>：排卡後的對話。用開放式問題，記錄參與者自己的解釋。</p><div class="q-two"><article><h3>你的+2陳述</h3><p data-endpoint="2">完成排序後，這裡會顯示你的陳述。</p><p>為甚麼這項最能表達你的觀點？</p></article><article><h3>你的−2陳述</h3><p data-endpoint="-2">完成排序後，這裡會顯示你的陳述。</p><p>為甚麼這項與你的觀點最不相符？</p></article></div><p class="lead small">哪項選擇最困難？中間位置代表甚麼？有沒有遺漏重要觀點？</p><p class="q-takeaway">完成後得到已保存的排列與參與者的理由。位置在每人的排序內具有相對意義。負分可表示相較其他陳述較不同意；零分可反映矛盾感受或較低的關注程度。</p><p><strong>對話示範：</strong>研究者：「為甚麼卡片1放+2？」學習者：「用英語時，我覺得自己是遊戲群組的一分子。」研究者：「可以談談一次這樣的經驗嗎？」用參與者代號保存理由。這段對話是假設例子。</p><p class="source-note">van Exel與de Graaf（2005）</p></div>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="Before analysis: save each person’s whole sort" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Analysis</span></header>

<div data-language="en"><h2>Turn each arrangement into a row of numbers.</h2><p class="lead small">Give each person a code, such as P01. For each card, record the column where they placed it. Card 1 at +2 becomes a score of +2. Save their explanation with the same participant code.</p><table class="q-data-table"><caption>One hypothetical sort in statement order</caption><thead><tr><th scope="row">Statement</th><th scope="col">1</th><th scope="col">2</th><th scope="col">3</th><th scope="col">4</th><th scope="col">5</th><th scope="col">6</th><th scope="col">7</th><th scope="col">8</th><th scope="col">9</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Score</th><td>+2</td><td>+1</td><td>0</td><td>−2</td><td>−1</td><td>+1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>−1</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="q-two"><article><h3>Check the record</h3><p>Every statement appears once. Scores match the grid. Entries match the saved layout.</p></article><article><h3>Compare complete Q-sorts</h3><p>Correlate each person’s whole sorting pattern with every other person’s pattern.</p></article></div><p class="q-takeaway">Q correlates participants’ complete sorts to identify shared viewpoints.</p><p class="source-note">Zabala (2014). Check the required row and column orientation before importing data.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>把每份排列記錄成一列數字。</h2><p class="lead small">為每人設代號，例如P01。逐張記錄卡片所在的欄：卡片1放在+2，就記+2。訪談解釋也使用同一參與者代號保存。</p><p class="q-array">陳述1至9的示例分數<br />+2, +1, 0, −2, −1, +1, 0, 0, −1</p><div class="q-two"><article><h3>核對記錄</h3><p>每項陳述出現一次，分數符合格網，輸入與已保存的排序一致。</p></article><article><h3>比較完整Q排序</h3><p>計算每人的整份排序與其他人的整份排序之間的相關。</p></article></div><p class="q-takeaway">關鍵是比較不同Q排序，而非問卷題項之間的相關。</p><p class="source-note">Zabala（2014）。匯入資料前核對軟件要求的列與欄方向。</p></div>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="6. See what comparing whole sorts means" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner"><header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Step 6 · See the patterns</span></header><div data-language="en"><h2>P01 and P02 placed most cards in the same way.</h2><div class="q-table-scroll"><table class="q-worked-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Person</th><th scope="col">1</th><th scope="col">2</th><th scope="col">3</th><th scope="col">4</th><th scope="col">5</th><th scope="col">6</th><th scope="col">7</th><th scope="col">8</th><th scope="col">9</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">P01</th><td>+2</td><td>+1</td><td>0</td><td>−2</td><td>−1</td><td>+1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>−1</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">P02</th><td>+2</td><td>+1</td><td>0</td><td>−2</td><td>0</td><td>+1</td><td>−1</td><td>0</td><td>−1</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">P03</th><td>−1</td><td>0</td><td>+1</td><td>+2</td><td>0</td><td>−2</td><td>0</td><td>−1</td><td>+1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Read each row as one person’s complete sort. P01 and P02 give the same positions to seven of the nine cards. P03 puts enjoyment (card 4) high and deliberate study (card 6) low.</p><p class="q-takeaway">This illustrates the comparison Q makes: similarities across whole arrangements. A real analysis checks all participants statistically before deciding which patterns to retain.</p><p class="source-note">Invented IDLE example for learning the procedure. These are not interview data or study findings.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>P01與P02把大部分卡片放在相同位置。</h2><div class="q-table-scroll"><table class="q-worked-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">參與者</th><th scope="col">1</th><th scope="col">2</th><th scope="col">3</th><th scope="col">4</th><th scope="col">5</th><th scope="col">6</th><th scope="col">7</th><th scope="col">8</th><th scope="col">9</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">P01</th><td>+2</td><td>+1</td><td>0</td><td>−2</td><td>−1</td><td>+1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>−1</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">P02</th><td>+2</td><td>+1</td><td>0</td><td>−2</td><td>0</td><td>+1</td><td>−1</td><td>0</td><td>−1</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">P03</th><td>−1</td><td>0</td><td>+1</td><td>+2</td><td>0</td><td>−2</td><td>0</td><td>−1</td><td>+1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>每一列是一個人的完整排序。P01與P02有七張卡的位置相同。P03則把享受（卡片4）排高、刻意學習（卡片6）排低。</p><p class="q-takeaway">這示範Q比較甚麼：整份排列的相似程度。正式分析需統計比較所有參與者，才決定保留哪些模式。</p><p class="source-note">為學習程序而設的IDLE假設例子，並非訪談資料或研究結果。</p></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="Step 6: find shared sorting patterns" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner"><header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Step 6 · Compare whole sorts</span></header>
<div data-language="en"><h2>Which people arranged the cards similarly?</h2><p class="lead">Compare each person’s complete arrangement with everyone else’s. A <strong>correlation</strong> is a number showing how similar two sorting patterns are.</p><ol class="q-analysis q-beginner-analysis"><li><strong>Prepare and import the data</strong><span>Open Ken-Q Analysis and use its import instructions or template. Enter the card statements in numbered order and each participant’s scores. Check the imported participant count, statement count and grid against your original records.</span></li><li><strong>Look for shared patterns</strong><span><strong>By-person factor analysis</strong> finds patterns across people’s whole sorts. A <strong>factor</strong> represents a shared viewpoint.</span></li><li><strong>Check which sorts support each pattern</strong><span>A <strong>factor loading</strong> shows how closely a person’s sort relates to a factor. Some sorts may relate to several factors or support none clearly.</span></li><li><strong>Build a summary arrangement</strong><span>Combine the sorts that clearly support each factor. This creates a <strong>factor array</strong>: an idealised card arrangement for that viewpoint.</span></li></ol><p class="q-takeaway">You finish with a small set of shared patterns and a summary card arrangement for each. The software does not tell you what the viewpoints mean.</p><details class="q-method-detail"><summary>When you are ready to run an analysis</summary><p>Learn how to choose extraction and rotation methods, compare the number of factors, and set criteria for defining sorts. Document these decisions with your supervisor. Options include centroid factor analysis or principal components analysis, and varimax or justified judgemental rotation.</p></details><p class="source-note"><a href="https://shawnbanasick.github.io/ken-q-analysis/">Open Ken-Q Analysis</a> · <a href="https://journal.r-project.org/articles/RJ-2014-032/">Zabala (2014): analysis and outputs</a></p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>哪些人的整份排列比較相似？</h2><p class="lead">把每人的完整排列與其他人比較。<strong>相關</strong>是一個表示兩份排序模式相似程度的數字。</p><ol class="q-analysis q-beginner-analysis"><li><strong>準備並匯入資料</strong><span>開啟Ken-Q Analysis，跟隨匯入指引或範本，按編號提供卡片陳述及各參與者分數。核對匯入的人數、卡片數及格網是否與原始記錄一致。</span></li><li><strong>尋找共同模式</strong><span><strong>以人為中心的因素分析</strong>尋找不同人的完整排序之間的模式；一個<strong>因素</strong>代表一種共同觀點。</span></li><li><strong>檢查哪些排序支持各模式</strong><span><strong>因素負荷量</strong>表示某人的排序與一個因素有多接近。有些排序可能與多個因素有關，也可能未清楚支持任何因素。</span></li><li><strong>建立一份總結排列</strong><span>綜合清楚支持同一因素的排序，建立<strong>因素排序陣列</strong>，即代表該觀點的理想化卡片排列。</span></li></ol><p class="q-takeaway">完成後得到幾種共同模式，以及每種模式的一份總結排列。軟件不會替你解釋觀點的意義。</p><details class="q-method-detail"><summary>準備實際分析時，再學這些決策</summary><p>學習選擇抽取與轉軸方法、比較保留因素數目，以及訂定界定排序的準則，與指導老師討論並記錄理據。選項包括重心因素分析或主成分分析，以及最大變異轉軸或有理據的判斷轉軸。</p></details><p class="source-note"><a href="https://shawnbanasick.github.io/ken-q-analysis/">開啟Ken-Q Analysis</a> · <a href="https://journal.r-project.org/articles/RJ-2014-032/">Zabala（2014）：分析與輸出</a></p></div>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="Step 7: explain and name each viewpoint" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Step 7 · Explain the patterns</span></header>

<div data-language="en"><h2>What story does each arrangement tell?</h2><p>Read the highest cards, the lowest cards and the participant explanations together. The columns below show parts of two <strong>factor arrays</strong>: summary arrangements for two hypothetical viewpoints.</p><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Statement</th><th scope="col">Connection and deliberate learning</th><th scope="col">Enjoyment comes first</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1. Wider community</th><td>+2</td><td>−1</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">4. Enjoyment as the main reason</th><td>−2</td><td>+2</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">6. Deliberate study of expressions</th><td>+1</td><td>−2</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">7. Confidence in online conversations</th><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="lead small">One pattern combines connection with language study. The other prioritises enjoyment and places deliberate study much lower.</p><p class="q-takeaway">Write a short description, support it with card positions and interview evidence, then choose a name. These examples are invented for teaching; real interpretation uses the entire array.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>每份排列呈現甚麼觀點？</h2><p>一起閱讀最高、最低的卡片及參與者解釋。下表兩欄是兩種假設觀點的<strong>因素排序陣列</strong>片段，即各觀點的總結排列。</p><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">陳述</th><th scope="col">連繫與刻意學習</th><th scope="col">享受優先</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1. 更廣泛社群</th><td>+2</td><td>−1</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">4. 主要為享受內容</th><td>−2</td><td>+2</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">6. 刻意學習表達</th><td>+1</td><td>−2</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">7. 網上對話的信心</th><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="lead small">一個模式結合社群連繫與語言學習；另一個優先重視享受，刻意研習則排得較低。</p><p class="q-takeaway">先寫簡短描述，用卡片位置與訪談證據支持，再選名稱。這些是教學假設例子；實際詮釋需檢視完整陣列。</p></div>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="Evidence for an interpretation" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Step 7 · Explain the patterns</span></header>

<div data-language="en"><h2>Support the viewpoint with evidence</h2><div class="q-two"><article><h3>Distinguishing statements</h3><p>Cards that help tell viewpoints apart. Check whether their factor scores differ statistically; a different displayed position alone is insufficient.</p></article><article><h3>Consensus statements</h3><p>Cards with no statistically significant differences across the retained viewpoints at the chosen threshold. They may be high, low or in the middle; “consensus” does not mean everyone agrees with the statement.</p></article></div><p class="q-takeaway">Use the full array, tested factor differences and interview explanations. Seek evidence that complicates your first interpretation.</p><p>Report the statement selection, recruitment, grid and instructions, analytical decisions, loadings and arrays. Explain what perspectives your design may have missed.</p><p class="source-note">Brown (1993); Zabala (2014). A factor label describes a viewpoint, not a permanent personality type.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>以證據支持觀點詮釋</h2><div class="q-two"><article><h3>區辨陳述</h3><p>幫助分辨不同觀點的卡片。檢查因素分數是否有統計顯著差異；只看顯示位置不同並不足夠。</p></article><article><h3>共識陳述</h3><p>在所選門檻下，各保留觀點之間沒有統計顯著差異的卡片。位置可以高、低或居中；「共識」不代表所有人都同意陳述。</p></article></div><p class="q-takeaway">結合完整陣列、經檢定的因素差異及訪談解釋，並尋找使初步詮釋更複雜的證據。</p><p>報告陳述選取、招募、格網與指示、分析決策、負荷量及排序陣列，也說明研究設計可能遺漏哪些觀點。</p><p class="source-note">Brown（1993）；Zabala（2014）。因素標籤描述觀點，而非固定人格類型。</p></div>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="Check your understanding" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Understanding check</span></header>

<div data-language="en"><h2>Three questions before you leave</h2><div class="q-questions"><details><summary>Can this small Q study tell us what percentage of all students share a viewpoint?</summary><p>No. Estimating prevalence requires an appropriate separate sampling and measurement design.</p></details><details><summary>What do we compare in Q analysis?</summary><p>Participants’ complete card arrangements, called Q-sorts. We compare how people placed all the cards together.</p></details><details><summary>What evidence supports naming a factor?</summary><p>The whole factor array, its differences from other factors, and relevant participants’ explanations.</p></details></div><p class="q-takeaway">Explain your answer to a partner before opening each response.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>離開前的三個問題</h2><div class="q-questions"><details><summary>這項小型Q研究能告訴我們全體學生中有多少比例持某觀點嗎？</summary><p>不能。估計普遍程度需要另設合適的抽樣及測量設計。</p></details><details><summary>Q分析比較甚麼？</summary><p>每位參與者的完整卡片排列，即Q排序。我們比較所有卡片一起排列的模式。</p></details><details><summary>為因素命名需要甚麼證據？</summary><p>完整因素陣列、與其他因素的差異，以及相關參與者的解釋。</p></details></div><p class="q-takeaway">先向同伴解釋，再打開各題答案。</p></div>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="Your first Q study" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Next steps</span></header>

<div data-language="en"><h2>Begin with your research question</h2><label class="q-write">A possible Q research question<textarea data-plan="question" rows="2" placeholder="What different viewpoints exist about…?"></textarea></label><label class="q-write">Two contrasting experiences to seek when recruiting<textarea data-plan="participants" rows="2" placeholder="Who might see this issue differently, and why?"></textarea></label><p class="source-note" data-plan-status>Notes stay in this browser and are not submitted.</p><p>Before collecting data: gather a wide range of opinions, choose and test the cards, explain whom you will invite, and plan consent and secure data storage.</p><p class="q-takeaway">For a dissertation, follow this introduction with methodological reading and supervised analysis practice.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>從自己的研究問題開始</h2><label class="q-write">一個可能的Q研究問題<textarea data-plan="question" rows="2" placeholder="圍繞某議題，存在哪些不同觀點？"></textarea></label><label class="q-write">招募時希望尋找的兩種不同經驗<textarea data-plan="participants" rows="2" placeholder="誰可能有不同看法？為甚麼？"></textarea></label><p class="source-note" data-plan-status>筆記只保留於此瀏覽器，並不提交。</p><p>收集資料前：蒐集廣泛意見，選卡並試做，說明邀請哪些人及原因，並安排知情同意與安全儲存。</p><p class="q-takeaway">用於學位論文前，需進一步閱讀方法文獻，並在指導下練習分析。</p></div>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="Reading and software" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Resources</span></header>

<div data-language="en"><h2>Where to go next</h2><p class="lead"><a href="https://qmethod.org/resources/how2q/">Q Methodology Society: Getting started with Q</a></p><p>Use this as your main resource hub for introductory videos, foundational readings, workshops and analysis tools.</p><ul class="q-resources"><li><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoXwsi2GLOc">Watch the introductory video linked by the Society</a><span>Begin with an overview of how a Q study works.</span></li><li><a href="https://qmethod.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/01/qmethodologyasneakpreviewreferenceupdate.pdf">Read van Exel &amp; de Graaf (2005): Q methodology — a sneak preview</a><span>Work through the design, sorting and interpretation guidance.</span></li><li><a href="https://youtube.com/channel/UCZ1pQYsO-DK8eV0NIDvDjSQ">Steven Brown’s workshops</a> · <a href="https://youtube.com/@zoomqseminar3306">Zoom Q seminar channel</a><span>Continue with the workshop and seminar collections listed by the Society.</span></li><li><a href="https://shawnbanasick.github.io/ken-q-analysis/">Ken-Q Analysis</a> · <a href="https://github.com/shawnbanasick/kade">KADE</a><span>For analysis practice after you understand the study design.</span></li></ul><details class="q-method-detail"><summary>Further reading and application examples</summary><ul class="q-resources"><li><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1747938X20302785">Using Q methodology: Sorting out subjectivity in educational research.</a></li><li><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780128188866000071">Understanding stakeholder perceptions in smart cities: Applying a Q methodology to the Smart Gusu project in China.</a><span>Section 7.4: Research method: Q methodology.</span></li><li><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780080449104004983">Q Method/Analysis.</a></li><li><a href="https://ojs.library.okstate.edu/osu/index.php/osub/article/view/9020">Brown (1993). A primer on Q methodology.</a><span>Operant Subjectivity, 16(3/4), 91–138.</span></li><li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1191/1478088705qp022oa">Watts &amp; Stenner (2005). Doing Q methodology.</a><span>Qualitative Research in Psychology, 2(1), 67–91.</span></li><li><a href="https://qmethod.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/01/qmethodologyasneakpreviewreferenceupdate.pdf">van Exel &amp; de Graaf (2005). Q methodology: A sneak preview.</a></li><li><a href="https://journal.r-project.org/articles/RJ-2014-032/">Zabala (2014). qmethod: A package to explore human perspectives.</a><span>The R Journal, 6(2), 163–173.</span></li><li><a href="https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.01360">Banasick (2019). KADE: A desktop application for Q methodology.</a><span>Journal of Open Source Software, 4(36), 1360.</span></li></ul><p><a href="https://shawnbanasick.github.io/ken-q-analysis/">Ken-Q Analysis</a> &nbsp; <a href="https://github.com/shawnbanasick/kade">KADE and documentation</a></p><p class="source-note">Adapted from the April 2025 Q methodology training and the 15-minute postgraduate teaching notes. All IDLE statements and factor examples are teaching illustrations; they are not findings from the linked studies.</p></details></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>下一步到哪裡學？</h2><p class="lead"><a href="https://qmethod.org/resources/how2q/">Q方法學會：Getting started with Q</a></p><p>以此作主要資源入口，尋找入門影片、基礎讀物、工作坊及分析工具。</p><ul class="q-resources"><li><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZoXwsi2GLOc">觀看學會連結的入門影片</a><span>先了解一項Q研究如何進行。</span></li><li><a href="https://qmethod.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/01/qmethodologyasneakpreviewreferenceupdate.pdf">閱讀van Exel與de Graaf（2005）：Q methodology — a sneak preview</a><span>逐步閱讀設計、排序及詮釋指引。</span></li><li><a href="https://youtube.com/channel/UCZ1pQYsO-DK8eV0NIDvDjSQ">Steven Brown工作坊</a> · <a href="https://youtube.com/@zoomqseminar3306">Zoom Q研討會頻道</a><span>繼續學習學會所列的工作坊及研討會資源。</span></li><li><a href="https://shawnbanasick.github.io/ken-q-analysis/">Ken-Q Analysis</a> · <a href="https://github.com/shawnbanasick/kade">KADE</a><span>理解研究設計後，用於練習分析。</span></li></ul><details class="q-method-detail"><summary>延伸閱讀與應用例子</summary><ul class="q-resources"><li><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1747938X20302785">Using Q methodology: Sorting out subjectivity in educational research.</a></li><li><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780128188866000071">Understanding stakeholder perceptions in smart cities: Applying a Q methodology to the Smart Gusu project in China.</a><span>Section 7.4: Research method: Q methodology.</span></li><li><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780080449104004983">Q Method/Analysis.</a></li><li><a href="https://ojs.library.okstate.edu/osu/index.php/osub/article/view/9020">Brown (1993). A primer on Q methodology.</a><span>Operant Subjectivity, 16(3/4), 91–138.</span></li><li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1191/1478088705qp022oa">Watts &amp; Stenner (2005). Doing Q methodology.</a><span>Qualitative Research in Psychology, 2(1), 67–91.</span></li><li><a href="https://qmethod.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/01/qmethodologyasneakpreviewreferenceupdate.pdf">van Exel &amp; de Graaf (2005). Q methodology: A sneak preview.</a></li><li><a href="https://journal.r-project.org/articles/RJ-2014-032/">Zabala (2014). qmethod: A package to explore human perspectives.</a><span>The R Journal, 6(2), 163–173.</span></li><li><a href="https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.01360">Banasick (2019). KADE: A desktop application for Q methodology.</a><span>Journal of Open Source Software, 4(36), 1360.</span></li></ul><p><a href="https://shawnbanasick.github.io/ken-q-analysis/">Ken-Q Analysis</a> &nbsp; <a href="https://github.com/shawnbanasick/kade">KADE and documentation</a></p><p class="source-note">根據2025年4月Q方法培訓及15分鐘研究生教學筆記改編。IDLE活動陳述與因素例子均僅供教學，並非連結研究的結果。</p></details></div>
</div></section>
